#{[#edb/ref 17592186048954 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060617} :activity/id #uuid "00da3ec7-77eb-5940-bbb1-30023abfe532" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060920 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ we know that the dot product can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cdot |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,| \\cdot \\cos\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle formed between the vectors when their tails are placed at the same point.\n\nHowever, there is another formula for the dot product that we can use if the two vectors are given in component form. If the components of our two vectors are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}a_x},{\\color{blue}a_y}, a_z \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}b_x},{\\color{blue}b_y}, b_z \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthen the dot product can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = {\\color{red}a_x} \\cdot {\\color{red}b_x} + {\\color{blue}a_y} \\cdot  {\\color{blue}b_y} + a_z \\cdot b_z\n$$\n\nFor example, if we have two vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = \\langle {\\color{red}3},{\\color{blue}-5},2 \\rangle,\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = \\langle {\\color{red}4},{\\color{blue}6},7 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nThen we can find the dot product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =12-30+14 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The procedure for computing the dot product works for vectors of any number of components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =12-30 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3,-5,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6,7,9⟩ & =3\\cdot 4+(-5)\\cdot 6+2\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =12-30+14+9 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059730 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059543 :question/id #uuid "1ab8245a-39fb-5633-b5ff-865ab17dbfb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4323" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 0,-0.4,11 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,5,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-0.4)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-2+22 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using the General Formula"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060921 :tutorial/content "To understand the reasoning behind the formula for the dot product of two vectors, it's helpful to write the two vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors and carry out the computation the long way.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the dot product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 3,-5 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,6 \\rangle.$\n\nExpressing the vectors in terms of their elementary unit vectors, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=3i-5j,\\,b=4i+6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using properties of the dot product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3i+(-5)j)\\cdot (4i+6j) \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)i\\cdot i+(3\\cdot 6)i\\cdot j+(-5\\cdot 4)j\\cdot i+(-5\\cdot 6)j\\cdot j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j}$ are perpendicular, meaning the angle between them is $\\theta=90^\\circ$ and consequently their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{90}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since the angle between a vector and itself is $\\theta=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot i & =|\\,i\\,|\\cdot |\\,i\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}j\\cdot j & =|\\,j\\,|\\cdot |\\,j\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}{0}^{∘} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}i\\cdot j & =j\\cdot i=0, \\\\ i\\cdot i & =j\\cdot j=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand our computation reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =(3\\cdot 4)\\overset{=1}{\\overset{⏞}{i\\cdot i}}+(3\\cdot 6)\\overset{=0}{\\overset{⏞}{i\\cdot j}}+(-5\\cdot 4)\\overset{=0}{\\overset{⏞}{j\\cdot i}}+(-5\\cdot 6)\\overset{=1}{\\overset{⏞}{j\\cdot j}} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)\\cdot 1+(3\\cdot 6)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 4)\\cdot 0+(-5\\cdot 6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =⟨3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4)+(-5\\cdot 6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Formula"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059731 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544 :question/id #uuid "dfc10727-c0d7-5b83-a4f6-afe3eb4831cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4324" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}$. Given that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, find the value of $t$." :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot t \\\\  & =-8-10+t \\\\  & =t-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given Two Perpendicular Vectors"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060619}}] :activity/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059900}] [#edb/ref 17592186050783 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065085} :activity/id #uuid "2334b80e-a2d6-550a-8293-e10100602247" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065435 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064093 :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064094 :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064095 :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Basic Trigonometric Identities" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064389}] [#edb/ref 17592186050898 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101591} :activity/id #uuid "0674df03-7c13-5be8-b206-8215c54adc2f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102029 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100608 :question/id #uuid "8ea17e06-1248-510c-9e71-c57da0cb0d02" :question/math-academy-id "e-14405" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100609 :question/id #uuid "1579f932-6e7f-5daa-b001-54a9f6c236ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14406" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\log^2_3 x }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{(\\log_3 x)^2 }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\log_3 x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 3}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\ln 3\\,\\textrm d u = \\dfrac 1 x \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3({log}_{3}⁡x)^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102030 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^4} \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step 1:**  Change the variable.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = \\ln x.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\textrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:**  Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe use the table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =6(2-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Definite Integrals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100610 :question/id #uuid "d2f49acf-ed91-510d-a0f1-41c50eeef9e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14422" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n&= \\int_{e}^{e^2} (\\ln x)^2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{1}^{2} u^2\\,\\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n&=\\left.\\dfrac13u^{3}\\right|_{1}^{2}\\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(2^3-1^3 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(8-1 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{7}{3}\t\t\n\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Logarithmic Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101029}] [#edb/ref 17592186048678 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139350} :activity/id #uuid "0a5bdaa9-fe87-516a-8c94-9bc96565f389" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139667 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to know the values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy *both* of the equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=-4 \\\\ 6x-y=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe call this a **system of linear equations**. The equations of the system define two straight lines, so we can visualize the system by plotting both lines on the same graph.\n\n![](images/59/59f16d57e74fd94fdab464eca22cea1271b19a3b78114446e31343cfabd15a12.png)\n\nThe solution of the system corresponds to the point where the two lines intersect. In this case, it is the point $(2,6)$, as shown above. So $x=2$ and $y=6$ form the **solution** to the system. We can write the solution as the ordered pair $(2, 6).$\n\nWhenever we find a solution to a system of equations, it is a good idea to double-check our result. To check that the solution $x=2,y=6$ is the correct solution, we substitute our solution into *both* of the equations. \n\nLet's check the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & \\,=\\,-4 \\\\ (2)-(6) & =-4 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we check the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & \\,=\\,6 \\\\ 6(2)-6 & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ and $y=6$ satisfied *both* equations, it is indeed the solution to the system of equations." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139351}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138083 :question/id #uuid "cbf28a0c-c34d-5855-b72e-4225a11710c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13129" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=8 \\\\ 2x-y=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines. \n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2).$ Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a System From Its Graph"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138429 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138084 :question/id #uuid "86771ced-afc0-5061-a532-bc2345a52ea8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13130" :question/problem "![](images/09/0915c99015922e27855d92ebb13756da634a8f10dd8fe3abbc016590f06e1077.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" :question/worked-solution "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400).$ So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days."} :knowledge-point/title "Word Problems"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138586}]}
